--TEST--
A contagious Core parameter accepts a tainted argument and carries the qualifier into the answer, while a neutral one accepts the same argument and answers plain -- ADR 0088 §§ 1-2
--FILE--
<?nvs
// ADR 0088 § 2: an unclassified `string` parameter refuses a tainted
// argument, so the two marks that *accept* are what this case pins.
// `Core\Str::join`'s separator is contagious -- its bytes decide which slice
// comes back -- so the answer carries whatever qualifier the argument had.
tainted string $t = "b" as tainted string;
tainted string $joined = Core\Str::join(["a", "c"], $t);
echo $joined, "\n";

// `Core\Str::length` is neutral: a count carries no byte of its subject, so
// the same argument is accepted and the answer is a plain `uint`.
uint $n = Core\Str::length($t);
echo $n, "\n";

// The contagion is a property of the argument and not of the member: the same
// call with nothing tainted in it still answers a plain `string`.
string $plain = Core\Str::join(["a", "c"], "b");
echo $plain, "\n";
--EXPECT--
abc
1
abc
